With the value $K = 4$, the #emph[corpus] was divided into 354 articles for training and
176 articles for testing, which are then processed for feature extraction with the TF-IDF
statistical measure. Fig. 37 below presents a piece of the code for the part that extracts the
features from the text files:

#fig("fig37.png", [Demonstration of feature extraction from the articles with the TF-IDF statistical measure.], width: 75%)

This resource creates the TF-IDF matrix, which computes the word count values, creates
a model based on the TF-IDF measures, and helps convert text data into numeric format, where
the vector value it generates is the product of those two terms. TfidfVectorizer provides a vector
named #emph[vectorizer]; so that it can be the feature model, the fit_transform function is
called, a transformer for fitting the data instances. This function is called by the
#emph[vectorizer] vector, which takes as a parameter the set of articles for training and testing
already divided by the k-folds method, these being iterable lists of strings in which each string
represents the contents of the documents contained in the #emph[corpus], the content of these
strings having a number of documents for each list. A short excerpt of the algorithm that forms
the classifier is shown in Fig. 38 [38, 119, 76].

#fig("fig38.png", [The function is called by the #emph[vectorizer] vector, which is the model
coming from the TfidfVectorizer function; this function takes X_train and X_test as parameters.], width: 75%)

The results generated by this fit_transform function are sparse matrices containing the
string of numbers of each document of the iterable list, being the word count weighted by the
TF-IDF statistical measure and the weights of the words per document of fixed length, for each
document in the training and test sets. This may also be called the term--document matrix [76].

After the texts contained in the #emph[corpus] have been processed into numeric format,
the SVM classifier can be trained, with its hyperparameter already adjusted by the grid search
technique. This classifier uses two parameters of the term--document matrix, these being
training and test together with the polarity classification labels originating from the
#emph[corpus]. The content of these labels, as mentioned, forms part of the manual
classification carried out by the human reviewers, which binds each article set aside for training
and for testing. Fig. 39 shows how the labels are used in the form of vectors.

#fig("fig39.png", [Extraction of the polarity labels of the classification.], width: 75%)

The training of the SVM classifier is carried out together with the k-fold technique, and
so are the TF-IDF weightings, not just the classifier. This can be explained by the fact that the
cross-validation method had to be implemented inside a loop, where at each validation iteration
both the TF-IDF part and the SVM part are processed.

At each iteration, the loop that makes up the cross-validation creates clones of the
classifier, trains those clones on the training part, and makes predictions on the test part,
counting the number of correct predictions and delivering their proportion through the confusion
matrix. This way of developing cross-validation is effective for gaining greater control over the
data to be classified [38]. Fig. 40 shows an excerpt of the implementation of the k-folds
technique in structured Portuguese (Portugol).

#fig("fig40.png", [Algorithm of the k-folds technique, written in structured algorithmic notation
(Portugol). Demonstration of the interactions of cross-validation with the classifier and the
TF-IDF method.])

The k-folds cross-validation with $K = 4$ --- that is, with 4 parts --- made it possible to
test all the files of the #emph[corpus], bearing in mind that, with the imbalance of this set of
articles, the classification was practically polarized in equal proportion (see Fig. 21), leading to
a fairer classification when obtaining the judgments for machine learning geared to the SR.

