== 4.4. Discussion of the results

With a total of 530 articles balanced and polarized as "relevant" and "not relevant," the number
of articles predicted correctly and the number of articles classified incorrectly by the classifier
are presented, and alongside them a comparison can be made between the balanced and the
unbalanced #emph[corpus] with their classifications.

#fig("fig42.png", [Confusion matrix with the balanced and unbalanced corpus and their metrics.])

As noted in subchapter 3.5.3, the confusion matrix exposes a great deal of information
that needs to be analyzed in more detail. Fig. 42 shows, on the left-hand side, the confusion
matrix with the classified and balanced #emph[corpus], and on the right-hand side the confusion
matrix with the classification of the unbalanced #emph[corpus]. Each row of the matrix
represents an actual class, while each column represents a predicted class.

The first row of the balanced confusion matrix considers the "not relevant" articles: 216
of them were correctly classified as "not relevant," which is called a true negative, TN. In the
second quadrant, on the same row as the first quadrant, 49 articles were wrongly classified as
"relevant," which is a false positive, FP. In the second row, that is in the third quadrant, the
"relevant" articles are considered: 33 articles were wrongly classified as "not relevant," called a
false negative, FN, while 232 articles were classified as "relevant," which is called a true
positive, TP.

One observation to be noted for a perfect classifier is that it would have only true
positives TP and true negatives TN, so that the confusion matrix would have non-zero values
only on its main diagonal (upper left to lower right). As can be seen, with this #emph[corpus]
used with the hyperparameter adjustment and balanced, an almost perfect classification was
obtained [38].

As observed in the matrix with the imbalanced #emph[corpus] in Fig. 42, at a ratio of
4.27 "not relevant" articles to 1 "relevant" article, it can be seen that the concentration of articles
as FN, false negative, in the imbalanced #emph[corpus] is very large, with 94 articles wrongly
classified as "not relevant."

Following the same steps as the example of the results for the balanced #emph[corpus],
it is notable that balancing the information contained in the dataset is important for obtaining
answers with greater precision, helping both with classification and with the organization of the
information.

It may be deduced that information, when it has a more balanced classification, yields as
a result greater precision and confidence when using the classifier on the data provided. This
can be seen in Table 4, which compares the metrics between the two types of #emph[corpus].

Tables 4 and 5 below present, with better visualization, the comparison of the metrics of
the SVM classifier with the balanced and the unbalanced dataset.

