As can be seen, there is a great difference between the two types of result for the two
states of the #emph[corpus]. The more balanced it is, the better the results will be, and the
quantity of information will be taken into account, yielding a better proportion in the results.

To make the reviewers' work easier by sparing them from reading the whole abstract,
which can be long, text summarization is used at this point in the classification of the articles.
This helps the researcher gain a better advance sense of the content of the abstract, making it
easier to know whether, even with the classification complete, that same article really would be
what the researcher wants --- bearing in mind that the supervised classifier proposed in this
dissertation does not replace the human work of making a more complete and precise
classification.

Fig. 43 shows the result of the summarization of the abstract of article Doc no. 16474;
this field is the original index that the reviewers gave to each article out of a total of 530
contained in the balanced #emph[corpus]. This summarization is based on the content analyzed
statistically by the LDA model with Mallet, and can be carried out with the help of the Gensim
library using the TextRank function.

